# PinkWrite 99 install

## Requirements
Linux, Apache (or nginx), PHP 8, MariaDB. Same class of host as WordPress.

## Steps
1. `git clone https://github.com/PinkWrite/99.git` into the web folder.
2. Create a database and user.
3. Visit `install.php`.
   - DB host: `127.0.0.1` (not `localhost`)
   - Public host: everything after `https://` (example `write.pink/99`)
4. Log in as the Superintendent. Create a Facility, then an Administrator.

## Packages
Arch/Debian/RPM builders live in [99-package](https://github.com/PinkWrite/99-package). Same installer: `contrib/pw99-install`. `--webroot` sets destination; interactive does not ask for it. Verb uses `ink install pw99 -d domain.tld`.

## Mail
Edit `config.php` (`mail` key). SMTP password lives there on purpose. This is SysAdmin work.

## Update / old dump
Do **not** run `install.php` on a live old database. That writes an empty schema.

1. Clone **master** into the web folder.
2. Create an empty MariaDB database. Import the old dump into it (not on top of `sql/schema.sql`).
3. Copy `config.sample.php` to `config.php`. Set `db.host` to `127.0.0.1`, plus name/user/pass, and `host` (scheme-less).
4. Lift once:

```
php bin/update.php
```

Same SQL, if you would rather run it by hand:

```
mysql -u USER -p DATABASE < sql/legacy-lift.sql
```

Old column names stay. New columns (`groups_json`, `editor_id`, `block_id`, `kind`, `facilities`, …) are added and copied. Then log in with an existing account.

## Nginx
```
location = /config.php { deny all; }
location ~ ^/(lib|sql|bin|functions|dev)/ { deny all; }
```
